 "name": "East Asia and the Pacific: how U.S. officials judged their persuasion, 1950-1977",
 "authorLine": "Agent run f7, 2026-10-31",
 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: the series never uses the phrase soft power, but its East Asia, Pacific and public-diplomacy volumes hold the officials' own judgments, under the older names information program, psychological strategy and public diplomacy. They show a 1950 programme of local-language material that its own officials called weak and badly aimed; a recurring belief that Asian audiences follow the winner, respect resolve and misread aid as softness; a view that American society and single events drew Asian audiences more than messages did; and, after Vietnam and the China opening, a message of reassurance that posts reported audiences did not credit until policy was clear. Where FRUS points for the rest: the printed documents came mostly from the State Department's central decimal files (Record Group 59) and from the Records of the U.S. Information Agency (Record Groups 306 and 305, Director's Subject Files and Office of Policy and Plans files), with presidential-library series for a few; the editors' footnotes point, unprinted, to State Department lot files and to presidential-library national security files. What this request cannot get from FRUS: independent measurement of how audiences received the messages, the content of the broadcasts, films and publications themselves, the wartime and Japan-occupation information work (not read in this run), and the Asian side of the exchange.",
