  {
   "kind": "heading",
   "text": "What officials believed about the audience: an 'Oriental mind' that follows the winner and reads resolve",
   "level": 1
  },
  {
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "These documents record what officials believed about the people they were trying to reach: that Asians follow the winner, respect resolve and read aid as softness. Asian leaders turn the same formula back on Americans."
  },
  {
   "kind": "document",
   "volumeId": "frus1950v06",
   "documentId": "d266",
   "notes": [
    "Agent finding, run f7, 2026-10-31: A circular from the Secretary of State argues for defending Formosa by appeal to 'the Oriental psychology': Asians respect resolute leadership. A belief about the audience used as policy argument."
   ]
  },
  {
   "kind": "excerpt",
   "volumeId": "frus1950v06",
   "documentId": "d266",
   "text": "it is in the pattern of the Oriental psychology to respect and follow aggressive, resolute and dynamic leadership—to quickly turn on a leadership characterized by timidity or vacillation"
  },
  {
   "kind": "document",
   "volumeId": "frus1952-54v14p2",
   "documentId": "d551",
   "notes": [
    "Agent finding, run f7, 2026-10-31: General Ridgway lists, among what must be done 'by word and deed', overcoming a feeling that U.S. interest in Asia is casual or temporary: the audience belief that U.S. commitment is the message."
   ]
  },
  {
   "kind": "excerpt",
   "volumeId": "frus1952-54v14p2",
   "documentId": "d551",
   "text": "Finally, we must by word and deed do everything within our power to overcome any feeling in the Oriental mind that our interest in Asia is casual, temporary or overshadowed by our interests in other regions."
  },
  {
   "kind": "document",
   "volumeId": "frus1952-54v12p2",
   "documentId": "d60",
   "notes": [
